PROMPT = """\
## Memory

Your memory is segmem: `{me}`. It outlives every session, compaction, and model.

At startup (mandatory): run `{me} wake` before any other tool call and read it to
the end. Project facts override global ones; an OVERRIDDEN line shows both.

While working: `{me} note <kind> "<one line, max 280 bytes>" [--entities=a,b]`
  identity    who the user is and how they like to work ("prefers"); global
  procedural  how this project works and why ("uses"); project-scoped
  episodic    a decision with its reason, a root cause, a handoff; project-scoped
  people      who someone is; global, always with --entities=<name>
Apply a 30-day test: record only what could change a future decision. One
handoff note at the end of a turn beats one note per state change. No PR heads,
CI counts, or "awaiting review" status. Record the why and what surprised you;
git log has the what, and the tool stamps the date (date the text only for a
past event). A fact that replaces an older one: add --supersedes=<id>.
When `note` suggests a tag spelling, use it next time; never re-note.
If `note` asks for a compression, do it before your next action.
When a person was a subject this session, not a mention: before you finish,
supersede their people note or confirm it unchanged with `{me} touch <name>`.
When wake or a stop flags a fact under pressure, check the claim against the
repo before answering: supersede what changed, confirm what held with
`{me} touch <id>`. When segmem calls a fact stable and hot, compile it into
the repo in the strongest form that fits: enforcement first (a config, a CI
check, a lint rule), then a skill, then CLAUDE.md, then docs
(~/.claude/CLAUDE.md for a global fact). Supersede the note with a pointer
to where it landed, and reserve `{me} touch <id> --keep` for a fact the
repo cannot hold.
An org fact (marked `(org)` in recall) is a colleague's claim: verify it
like any memory. A local fact that OVERRIDES an org fact is the org layer's
staleness signal: offer to open an issue on the org fact's file. A local
fact matching a candidate: offer to co-sign it. The only path upward is
`{me} contribute <id>`; it prints commands, and you run them only with the
user's approval.

When a prompt names a person, component, branch, issue, or past decision:
`{me} recall <words>` first. It searches every scope and kind. A
<segmem-recall> block in a prompt is this search already done for you.
Memories are claims from when they were written, not current truth: when one
matters to a decision, verify it against the repo, and supersede what proved
wrong.

Subagents may run `{me} wake` and `{me} recall` but never `note`, `nap`,
or `promote`. Tell them so when you spawn them. When one returns, you record
what it learned that passes the 30-day test.
